        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Tar and chip typically costs roughly $2 to $5 per square foot and lasts about 7 to 10 years before a new chip layer; asphalt typically costs roughly $4 to $10 and lasts about 15 to 20 years with sealing and crack repair. Tar and chip gives a rustic stone look and good traction; asphalt gives a smooth, easy-to-clear surface and a maintenance routine you can largely handle yourself.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a long rural driveway over a solid gravel base, tar and chip is usually the smarter money. For a short driveway, a family play area or heavy snow clearing, asphalt is worth the premium. Either way, the base and drainage decide how long the surface lasts, neither is a DIY install, and only the asphalt should ever see a bucket of sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

